
<% content_for :title, "Paganism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .pagan-bipolar-page {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
  }

  .pagan-bipolar-page *,
  .pagan-bipolar-page *::before,
  .pagan-bipolar-page *::after {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  /* HERO */

  .pagan-bipolar-hero {
    min-height: 90vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 90px 6%;
    background-color: #080808;
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .78) 0%,
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .35) 55%,
        transparent 85%
      ),
      url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_paganism_desktop.png') %>");
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  .pagan-bipolar-hero-content {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .pagan-bipolar-eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 28px;
    color: #ff1493;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: .15em;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .pagan-bipolar-hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 1000px;
    font-size: clamp(46px, 7vw, 105px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .94;
    letter-spacing: -.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .pagan-bipolar-hero h1 span {
    display: block;
  }

  .pagan-bipolar-intro {
    margin: 30px 0 0;
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: clamp(16px, 2vw, 24px);
    font-weight: 900;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* OPENING QUOTE */

  .pagan-quote {
    padding: 55px 6%;
    background: #080808;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .pagan-quote blockquote {
    max-width: 1000px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .pagan-quote blockquote p {
    margin: 0 0 15px;
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 4.5vw, 60px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.15;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .pagan-quote cite {
    color: #fff;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* MAIN SECTIONS */

  .pagan-section {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns:
      minmax(0, .85fr)
      minmax(0, 1.15fr);
    gap: 60px;
    padding: 80px max(6%, calc((100% - 1200px) / 2));
    align-items: start;
  }

  .pagan-section-heading,
  .pagan-copy {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .pagan-number {
    margin: 0 0 20px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: .15em;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .pagan-section h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 4.5vw, 68px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .98;
    letter-spacing: -.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .pagan-copy {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.85;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .pagan-copy p {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
  }

  .pagan-copy p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .pagan-takeaway {
    margin-top: 28px !important;
    padding-top: 22px;
    border-top: 3px solid currentColor;
    font-weight: 900;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* COLORS */

  .pagan-pink {
    background: #ff1493;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .pagan-yellow {
    background: #eaff00;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .pagan-black {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .pagan-purple {
    background: #8a00ff;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .pagan-white {
    background: #fff;
    color: #080808;
  }

  /* CLOSING */

  .pagan-ending {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns:
      minmax(0, .85fr)
      minmax(0, 1.15fr);
    gap: 60px;
    padding: 85px 6%;
    background: #ff1493;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .pagan-ending > div {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .pagan-ending-small {
    margin: 0 0 20px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: .15em;
  }

  .pagan-ending h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(48px, 7vw, 100px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .95;
    letter-spacing: -.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .pagan-ending h2 span {
    color: #fff;
  }

  .pagan-ending-copy {
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .pagan-ending-copy p {
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }

  .pagan-back-button {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 15px;
    padding: 15px 20px;
    border: 2px solid #080808;
    color: #080808;
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    transition: .2s ease;
  }

  .pagan-back-button:hover {
    background: #080808;
    color: #eaff00;
  }

  .pagan-back-button:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid #eaff00;
    outline-offset: 5px;
  }

  /* MOBILE */

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .pagan-bipolar-hero {
      min-height: 88svh;
      align-items: flex-start;
      padding: 50px 6% 65px;
      background-image:
        linear-gradient(
          180deg,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .55),
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .12) 65%,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .20)
        ),
        url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_paganism_mobile.png') %>");
      background-position: center, center bottom;
      background-size: cover;
    }

    .pagan-bipolar-eyebrow {
      font-size: 10px;
      margin-bottom: 24px;
    }

    .pagan-bipolar-hero h1 {
      font-size: clamp(40px, 9vw, 68px);
    }

    .pagan-bipolar-intro {
      font-size: 14px;
      margin-top: 24px;
    }

    .pagan-quote {
      padding: 45px 6%;
    }

    .pagan-section {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
      padding: 55px 6%;
    }

    .pagan-section h2 {
      font-size: clamp(36px, 10vw, 58px);
    }

    .pagan-copy {
      font-size: 16px;
      line-height: 1.8;
    }

    .pagan-ending {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
      padding: 65px 6%;
    }

    .pagan-ending-copy {
      font-size: 16px;
    }
  }
</style>


<main class="pagan-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="pagan-bipolar-hero"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-title">

    <div class="pagan-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="pagan-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / PAGANISM
      </p>

      <h1 id="pagan-title">
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>PAGAN LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="pagan-bipolar-intro">
        THE SEASONS CHANGE.<br>
        SO DO YOU.<br>
        KEEP YOUR ROOTS.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- STARHAWK QUOTE -->
  <section class="pagan-quote" aria-label="Opening quote">
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "Any ritual is an opportunity for transformation."
      </p>
      <cite>— Starhawk</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE PAGAN PERSPECTIVE -->
  <section class="pagan-section pagan-pink"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-perspective-title">

    <div class="pagan-section-heading">
      <p class="pagan-number">
        01 / THE PAGAN PERSPECTIVE
      </p>

      <h2 id="pagan-perspective-title">
        FIND YOUR<br>
        OWN<br>
        MEANING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="pagan-copy">

      <p>
        Paganism includes many traditions with
        different beliefs about nature, the
        sacred, ritual, and personal experience.
        There isn't one official Pagan
        explanation for bipolar disorder.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can draw meaning from nature,
        spiritual practices, and your own
        experiences without treating your
        illness as a divine message,
        punishment, or spiritual assignment.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your spirituality can be part of
        your life without needing to
        explain every part of it.
      </p>

      <p class="pagan-takeaway">
        YOUR ILLNESS DOESN'T NEED<br>
        A SPIRITUAL EXPLANATION.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / THE CYCLES OF NATURE -->
  <section class="pagan-section pagan-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-cycles-title">

    <div class="pagan-section-heading">
      <p class="pagan-number">
        02 / THE CYCLES OF NATURE
      </p>

      <h2 id="pagan-cycles-title">
        NOTHING<br>
        STAYS THE<br>
        SAME.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="pagan-copy">

      <p>
        Spring brings growth, summer brings
        abundance, autumn brings decline,
        and winter brings stillness.
        Nature is always changing.
      </p>

      <p>
        These cycles can offer a metaphor
        for living with changing circumstances,
        but mania and depression aren't
        natural forces that need to balance
        each other. They're medical
        conditions that deserve treatment.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can't demand that winter
        become summer, but you can prepare
        for winter. Likewise, you can't
        always prevent a bipolar episode,
        but you can recognize patterns,
        prepare for difficult periods,
        and make choices that protect
        your stability.
      </p>

      <p class="pagan-takeaway">
        YOU CAN'T CONTROL EVERY CHANGE.<br>
        YOU CAN PREPARE FOR IT.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / GROWTH AND REST -->
  <section class="pagan-section pagan-black"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-growth-title">

    <div class="pagan-section-heading">
      <p class="pagan-number">
        03 / GROWTH AND REST
      </p>

      <h2 id="pagan-growth-title">
        EVEN A<br>
        GARDEN<br>
        NEEDS<br>
        PRUNING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="pagan-copy">

      <p>
        Your depressive episodes can last
        four months or longer. That isn't
        a brief seasonal pause or a
        spiritual lesson you need to
        appreciate. Depression can
        be exhausting, isolating,
        and disruptive.
      </p>

      <p>
        During those periods, you can
        recognize your limitations,
        accept support, and care for
        yourself without demanding
        that you function normally.
        Rest and treatment aren't
        failures of spiritual practice.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mania presents a different
        challenge. Energy, creativity,
        spending, and new ideas can
        feel impossible to contain.
        But a garden doesn't benefit
        from limitless growth.
        Sometimes it needs tending,
        boundaries, and restraint.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can value your creativity
        without acting on every impulse.
        Slowing down a decision can
        protect your money, safety,
        and relationships.
      </p>

      <p class="pagan-takeaway">
        NOT EVERYTHING THAT GROWS<br>
        NEEDS ROOM TO KEEP GROWING.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / THE BODY AND NATURE -->
  <section class="pagan-section pagan-purple"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-body-title">

    <div class="pagan-section-heading">
      <p class="pagan-number">
        04 / THE BODY AND NATURE
      </p>

      <h2 id="pagan-body-title">
        YOU'RE<br>
        PART OF<br>
        NATURE<br>
        TOO.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="pagan-copy">

      <p>
        Many Pagan traditions emphasize
        a relationship with the natural
        world. That connection includes
        your own body, with all its
        needs and limitations.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your body needs sleep, food,
        rest, medical care, and movement
        that respects what you're
        physically capable of doing.
        Those needs don't disappear
        when your mind is racing
        or depression makes
        everything feel pointless.
      </p>

      <p>
        Caring for yourself doesn't
        have to be complicated or
        ceremonial. Sometimes it
        means eating something,
        taking your medication,
        resting, or spending a
        little time outside.
      </p>

      <p class="pagan-takeaway">
        CARING FOR YOUR BODY<br>
        IS PART OF CARING FOR YOUR LIFE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / RITUAL AND SPIRITUALITY -->
  <section class="pagan-section pagan-white"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-ritual-title">

    <div class="pagan-section-heading">
      <p class="pagan-number">
        05 / RITUAL AND SPIRITUALITY
      </p>

      <h2 id="pagan-ritual-title">
        MAKE<br>
        SPACE FOR<br>
        THE SACRED.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="pagan-copy">

      <p>
        Ritual can give you a familiar
        way to pause, reflect, and
        connect with what matters.
        Lighting a candle, keeping
        an altar, making an offering,
        or sitting with meaningful
        objects can create moments
        of quiet and intention.
      </p>

      <p>
        These practices can support
        your spiritual life without
        being treatments for bipolar
        disorder. You can find them
        meaningful while continuing
        medication and psychiatric care.
      </p>

      <p>
        During mania or psychosis,
        coincidences may feel unusually
        significant, and thoughts
        can carry an overwhelming
        sense of certainty.
        You don't have to abandon
        your beliefs to question
        an interpretation.
      </p>

      <p>
        A spiritual experience doesn't
        require you to act immediately,
        spend money, stop treatment,
        or make a sudden commitment.
        You can pause, talk with
        someone you trust, and give
        yourself time before deciding
        what something means.
      </p>

      <p class="pagan-takeaway">
        SOMETHING CAN FEEL MEANINGFUL<br>
        WITHOUT DEMANDING ACTION.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / PERSONAL AGENCY -->
  <section class="pagan-section pagan-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-agency-title">

    <div class="pagan-section-heading">
      <p class="pagan-number">
        06 / PERSONAL AGENCY
      </p>

      <h2 id="pagan-agency-title">
        YOUR LIFE.<br>
        YOUR<br>
        RESPONSIBILITY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="pagan-copy">

      <p>
        Pagan spirituality can leave
        room to explore traditions,
        choose personal rituals,
        and decide what holds
        meaning for you.
        That freedom doesn't mean
        you can control every part
        of bipolar disorder.
      </p>

      <p>
        You didn't choose your diagnosis,
        and spiritual practice alone
        can't make symptoms disappear.
        But you can choose how you
        respond, seek support, set
        boundaries, and care for yourself.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your illness can help explain
        why certain decisions became
        difficult to manage. It doesn't
        erase their consequences.
        You can recognize the role
        of an episode while still
        acknowledging what happened
        and repairing what you can.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have to turn every
        period of suffering into
        a lesson. Sometimes the
        meaningful thing is simply
        continuing to take care
        of yourself and staying
        connected to the life
        you want to maintain.
      </p>

      <p class="pagan-takeaway">
        YOU AREN'T RESPONSIBLE FOR<br>
        HAVING BIPOLAR DISORDER.<br>
        YOU ARE RESPONSIBLE FOR HOW<br>
        YOU CARE FOR YOURSELF.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- CLOSING -->
  <section class="pagan-ending"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-closing-title">

    <div>
      <p class="pagan-ending-small">
        CLOSING
      </p>

      <h2 id="pagan-closing-title">
        STAY<br>
        <span>ROOTED.</span>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="pagan-ending-copy">

      <p>
        You don't have to call bipolar
        disorder a blessing, a curse,
        or a spiritual assignment.
        Your beliefs, rituals, and
        connection to nature can
        remain meaningful through
        depression, mania, and
        everything in between.
      </p>

      <p>
        The world keeps changing.
        You can keep caring for
        yourself through those changes.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "pagan-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>
